Date: Mon, 18 Jan 93 12:56:03 EST
From: lubkin@apollo.hp.com (David Lubkin)
Subject: Re: Immortalist conservatism

    Some people have claimed that immortalists should be very conservative
    about taking life-threatening risks, since an immortalist who risks death
    risks giving up an eternity of life.  The weaknesses of this argument have
    been well documented by actual immortalists who post to this list.
    :
    When a deathist considers a long-term commitment, she must take account of
    the fact that her choice may well constrain the rest of her life.
    :
    An immortalist, on the other hand, can afford to take greater risks with
    regard to long term commitments.

I try to live my life as a balance between pessimism and optimism.  I have
four plausible life spans.

1.  Abrupt.  I die today/soon.  Accident or AIDS.

2.  Natural.  I live to 70-120.  Diet, exercise, and supplements can push me 
    toward the outer edge.  This span will likely occur if either life 
    extension takes longer to achieve than we think or society collapses in 
    the interim.

3.  Extended.  I live 100's to 1000's of years.  I live until an accident
    gets me.

4.  Indefinite.  I live indefinitely long.  Requires that a download or 
    copy of me is still me.

Based on what I now want to do with my life, I have an intense interest in an
extended lifespan.  I have no particular use for an indefinite lifespan, nor
am I convinced that one is possible for me (because I don't think a copy or 
download is still me), but I am not ruling out the possibility.

Meanwhile, I don't know which of these life spans I will have.  So I try
to choose actions that make sense no matter which one happens, noting
especially that I am old enough (34) that if I may not make it to when #3
is possible.  If the group consensus is right and it's 2030, I probably
will.  If it's 2070, I probably won't.

As for Tom's remarks:  Just because I'm want to live a long time doesn't mean 
that I will.  There's quite a difference between being an immortalist 
and being immortal.  My actions should reflect the expected payoff, not my
hopes and dreams.

Luckily, there aren't many contradictions between living for the moment
and living for the future, at least not for me.  I >would< rather eat
fresh asparagus than chocolate layer cake.  

There are some risky activities I have chosen to defer, e.g., skydiving, 
visiting Bosnia, and unprotected satyriasis, largely because they aren't that
important to me and I can always do them when the risks are reduced.  On the 
other hand, I do fly (because it's important to me, and the risks are largely 
under my control), and I would risk immortality for a ride on the space 
shuttle.

    :
    Your immortalist friends and lovers will be with you a *long* time! 
    :

Which the squabblers on this list and CRYONET should keep in mind.
Don't burn any bridges and learn to forgive.


-- David Lubkin.

