Date: Mon, 18 Jan 93 12:44:09 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: Virtual comics

Last Sunday's CHICAGO TRIBUNE printed a color copy of the comic strip
"Dilbert" that involved internet.  I wasn't able to carry the strip away
then, and can't find it now, but here's the gist of the "plot":  Our geeky
hero is seen in the first panel falling into his computer terminal, crying
out "Help!  I'm being sucked into cyberspace!"  His little doggy sidekick
seizes the leg of his pants, but in the next panel we see that our hero has
apparently slipped out of his trousers.  His dog is left holding them, and
comments "Gee, I hope he won't need his pants in there."  

Subsequent panels show our partially-clothed hero floating through the
net's virtual realm, remarking in awe at the vast flow of data and the
complex web of human relationships swirling about him.  The last panel
shows him back in the real world describing his adventure to a lady friend:
". . . and so there I was in cyberspace without my pants--"  She
interrupts, saying, "Don't tell me; you're a geek, aren't you?"  

Notably, the comic strip's author included his net address in the last
panel!  I'm pleased to see that he is technically literate, as his comic
has long been one of my favorites.  It often meets the "laugh out loud"
criterion.


